import "core"


# An [if] whose two branches produce different values is just an [if], not a disagreement.
  This is a regression test for a gap where such an [if] derived an overlap-agreement test pairing
  its two branches against each other and reporting that they disagree — asserting, in effect, that
  the [if] must not branch.
  @deriveOverlapTests@ pairs same-name definitions whose domains intersect. The [if] declaration
  preprocessor rewrites the arm into a generated function of one argument —
  @/Catln/ThenElse/fromBool(v= <the condition>)@ — with one arm per branch. Those arms ought to have
  disjoint domains, since a [/Catln/ThenElse] is one member or the other and never both. They did
  not: each branch's [Then]/[Else] pattern was written as the ALIAS of a hole, and
  @TypeCheck/Encode@ bounds an alias's base by the argument variable the alias NAMES rather than by
  the alias's type — so the constructor constrained nothing, both arms sat at ⊤, and their domains
  intersected. The pattern now goes in the argument position, where it destructures as any other
  argument pattern does.
  The same fix is what makes @caseGuardWrongBranch.ct@ select correctly; both were the branch
  preprocessors putting a pattern somewhere it could not constrain anything.
greet(name -> String, formal -> Boolean) -> String= if(formal)
  Then= "Dear " ++ name

  Else= "Hi " ++ name

# That no agreement test is derived for [greet] is asserted by the test engine's own tests
  (@test/Eval/QuantificationTests.hs@); what is held here is that the branch is taken.
main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= greet(name= "Ada", formal= True) == "Dear Ada")
  #assert(test= greet(name= "Ada", formal= False) == "Hi Ada")
  io
